WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.871
<v Call-Line Supervisor>I pride myself on reading people. When a worker comes

00:00:02.871 --> 00:00:05.742
<v Call-Line Supervisor>in upset, I can usually tell what is going on

00:00:05.742 --> 00:00:07.464
<v Call-Line Supervisor>and get them back on track.

00:00:07.814 --> 00:00:12.482
<v Human Services Program Manager>That is a common belief. Researchers tested it in 25

00:00:12.482 --> 00:00:17.150
<v Human Services Program Manager>experiments. They asked people to imagine another person's perspective and

00:00:17.150 --> 00:00:21.818
<v Human Services Program Manager>then predict that person's feelings or preferences. Imagining did not

00:00:21.818 --> 00:00:26.486
<v Human Services Program Manager>improve accuracy. It slightly lowered it, and sometimes raised confidence.

00:00:26.836 --> 00:00:28.756
<v Call-Line Supervisor>So I might be confidently wrong.

00:00:29.106 --> 00:00:33.699
<v Human Services Program Manager>Possibly. What did work was asking. When people got the

00:00:33.699 --> 00:00:38.292
<v Human Services Program Manager>other person's perspective through conversation, accuracy went up. Imagination uses

00:00:38.292 --> 00:00:42.426
<v Human Services Program Manager>what you already believe. Asking brings in something new.

00:00:42.776 --> 00:00:45.650
<v Call-Line Supervisor>Last week a worker came in after a hard call.

00:00:45.650 --> 00:00:48.524
<v Call-Line Supervisor>I assumed the caller's anger had rattled her, so I

00:00:48.524 --> 00:00:51.398
<v Call-Line Supervisor>talked about handling angry callers. On her way out she

00:00:51.398 --> 00:00:54.273
<v Call-Line Supervisor>mentioned she was not sure about a phone number she

00:00:54.273 --> 00:00:54.560
<v Call-Line Supervisor>gave.

00:00:54.910 --> 00:00:58.030
<v Human Services Program Manager>That is the pattern. The number was the real concern,

00:00:58.030 --> 00:01:01.150
<v Human Services Program Manager>and possibly a risk for the caller that night. If

00:01:01.150 --> 00:01:04.270
<v Human Services Program Manager>you had asked what was hardest about the call, you

00:01:04.270 --> 00:01:05.830
<v Human Services Program Manager>might have heard it first.

00:01:06.180 --> 00:01:10.315
<v Call-Line Supervisor>What about emotional intelligence training? My team was offered an

00:01:10.315 --> 00:01:11.556
<v Call-Line Supervisor>assessment and workshop.

00:01:11.906 --> 00:01:16.651
<v Human Services Program Manager>Be careful with big claims. A meta-analysis of 58 studies

00:01:16.651 --> 00:01:21.397
<v Human Services Program Manager>found training raises scores moderately. But effects on actual behavior

00:01:21.397 --> 00:01:26.142
<v Human Services Program Manager>at work are largely unknown. And one meta-analysis found the

00:01:26.142 --> 00:01:30.888
<v Human Services Program Manager>link between emotional intelligence and leadership was 0.05 for ability

00:01:30.888 --> 00:01:32.786
<v Human Services Program Manager>tests, close to zero.

00:01:33.136 --> 00:01:35.008
<v Call-Line Supervisor>So it is mostly hype?

00:01:35.358 --> 00:01:39.358
<v Human Services Program Manager>Not entirely. Emotional skills do seem to matter more in

00:01:39.358 --> 00:01:43.358
<v Human Services Program Manager>jobs with heavy emotional demands, like your call line. The

00:01:43.358 --> 00:01:47.358
<v Human Services Program Manager>useful part is specific skills you practice, with feedback. Asking

00:01:47.358 --> 00:01:49.758
<v Human Services Program Manager>before advising is one of them.

00:01:50.108 --> 00:01:53.076
<v Call-Line Supervisor>My staff hear distress all day. I worry that asking

00:01:53.076 --> 00:01:55.748
<v Call-Line Supervisor>them to be more empathic just wears them out.

00:01:56.098 --> 00:02:00.027
<v Human Services Program Manager>That worry has support. In 11 studies, people avoided empathy

00:02:00.027 --> 00:02:03.955
<v Human Services Program Manager>because it felt effortful. But the avoidance disappeared when they

00:02:03.955 --> 00:02:07.884
<v Human Services Program Manager>felt they could empathize effectively. Give your staff concrete questions

00:02:07.884 --> 00:02:11.813
<v Human Services Program Manager>to ask and show them when their response helped. That

00:02:11.813 --> 00:02:14.170
<v Human Services Program Manager>makes empathy feel worth the effort.

00:02:14.520 --> 00:02:17.870
<v Call-Line Supervisor>So for my next check-in: ask what was hardest, listen,

00:02:17.870 --> 00:02:21.219
<v Call-Line Supervisor>then separate support for the worker from the steps that

00:02:21.219 --> 00:02:22.224
<v Call-Line Supervisor>protect the caller.

00:02:22.574 --> 00:02:26.710
<v Human Services Program Manager>And name who verifies the fact and who calls back,

00:02:26.710 --> 00:02:29.606
<v Human Services Program Manager>by when. Empathy and accuracy work together.
